Description

This lab demonstrates how to identify a column that supports string data using a SQL Injection UNION attack. After determining the correct number of columns, each column is tested individually by inserting a string while keeping the remaining columns as NULL.

Methodology
Determined the correct number of columns.
Used UNION SELECT with NULL values.
Replaced one NULL at a time with a string ('a').
Observed the application's response to identify the column that displayed the injected string.
Replaced the test string with the lab-provided value to successfully solve the lab.
Example Payloads
' UNION SELECT 'a',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL--
' UNION SELECT NULL,'a',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL--
' UNION SELECT NULL,NULL,'a',NULL,NULL,NULL--
Skills Practiced
SQL Injection
UNION-based SQL Injection
Column Enumeration
Data Type Identification
Burp Suite Repeater
Manual Web Application Testing
Key Takeaways
UNION queries must have the same number of columns.
NULL values simplify data type matching.
Only columns compatible with string data can display text-based information.
Identifying a text-compatible column is an essential step before extracting sensitive data during authorized penetration testing.
